// Position matrix (design 235), grid 1 "Adoption" — row: the repeat literal
// `[v; N]`, source S3 (const expression). The bare-literal (S1) row is
// already covered by the saw-lang skill's `[v; N]` examples and by
// `[0; 256]`-shaped uses throughout the corpus; this file is the const-
// expression case at a fixed element width, for an IN-RANGE value.
//
// The element genuinely adopts `UInt32` now: the adoption funnel folds a const
// expression and stamps the slot's type on it, so the value is stored at the
// declared width rather than carried at platform width past it. The
// out-of-range side of the same row is pinned by
// const_expression_range_checked_wide.saw. This row's small in-range value (24)
// printed correctly either way, which is exactly why the gap went unnoticed.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// repeat elem = 24
// repeat len = 4

func main() {
    let r: [UInt32; 4] = [(1 << 3) | (1 << 4); 4]
    print("repeat elem = {r[0]}")
    print("repeat len = {r.len()}")
}
